F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.03 to +0.24), about +1.6\u00b0F since 1903. Lumberton's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.04\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.12 to +0.04) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1979, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+1.64\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +1.37 to +1.92) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.75\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.52 to +0.97). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 5 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; January and September fell.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Lumberton's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.19 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 45.5 inches a year. The wettest year was 1924 (65.74 in); the driest, 2007 (22.75 in). Lumberton almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 9 daily record highs, 2 daily record lows"}}